id: pm-0wfdim
title: "GH-1036/GH-1040: history integrity verdicts and repairs are incompatible across supported CLI versions"
description: "Different supported pm versions report opposite hash-chain verdicts over identical workspace bytes, and each version recommends a repair that makes the other version reject more streams."
type: Issue
status: closed
priority: 0
tags[9]: "area:history","area:sdk",compatibility,context-integrity,durability,github-1036,github-1040,integrity,regression
created_at: "2026-08-16T05:11:38.233Z"
updated_at: "2026-08-16T08:57:46.900Z"
closed_at: "2026-08-16T08:57:46.206Z"
completed_at: "2026-08-16T08:57:46.206Z"
author: "harness:codex"
estimated_minutes: 600
acceptance_criteria: "Reproduce cross-version validation and repair with real released binaries and versioned fixtures; define and persist an explicit history item-hash format capability; make unsupported formats report unknown/incompatible rather than corrupt; refuse repair/reconcile when the running binary cannot safely write the detected format; preserve validation of legacy v1 and current v2 streams across the documented compatibility window; expose typed SDK, CLI, health, validate, merge, and recovery receipts; add unit, integration, cross-version subprocess, packed-consumer, and negative-control gates; document migration and support policy."
goal: project management = context management
objective: Make history integrity a version-independent project fact and prevent self-confirming destructive repairs.
value: "Pinned CI, global CLIs, and external packages agree whether immutable project history is valid before any mutation."
impact: A healthy workspace can be diagnosed as corrupt and repaired into a form that breaks its authoritative CI gate.
outcome: History format capability negotiation and repair preconditions keep every supported CLI version truthful and safe.
why_now: GitHub #1040 provides symmetric evidence that 2026.8.7 and 2026.8.15 disagree on identical bytes; GitHub #1036 retracts merge attribution and points to this root cause.
parent: pm-o2kc
risk: critical
confidence: high
severity: critical
environment: Identical tracker bytes read and repaired by pm 2026.8.7 and 2026.8.15
repro_steps: "Validate one committed workspace with both released binaries, repair with the newer binary, then validate the unchanged repaired bytes with both versions."
resolution: "History verification and repair preserve legacy and transitional unversioned prefixes, make a supported explicit hash epoch authoritative only from its marker forward, and prevent later ambiguous entries from downgrading it."
expected_result: "Verification accepts valid legacy and transitional prefixes before an explicit epoch marker, then keeps that marked epoch authoritative for all later unversioned entries and repair selection."
actual_result: "History verification and repair preserve legacy and transitional unversioned prefixes, make a supported explicit hash epoch authoritative only from its marker forward, and prevent later ambiguous entries from downgrading it."
